From: buildroot_optee
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: dts: qemu_rv64_virt_domain: add TPM TIS node on the platform bus

QEMU's riscv virt machine places dynamic sysbus devices such as
"-device tpm-tis-device,tpmdev=tpm0" on the platform bus at 0x4000000,
but only describes them in its own generated FDT.  When the guest is
booted with a user supplied "-dtb" (as the OP-TEE domain setup needs),
QEMU skips that and the DTB must carry the node itself.

The node matches what QEMU generates (dtc -I dtb of "-machine
dumpdtb=") for the first platform-bus slot.

The TCG2 event log buffer is described with "memory-region" pointing
at a /reserved-memory node, which both sides understand:
 - U-Boot's measured boot (lib/tpm_tcg2.c tcg2_platform_get_log())
   writes the log there; without a log buffer bootm_measure() silently
   measures nothing.
 - Linux (drivers/char/tpm/eventlog/of.c tpm_read_log_memory_region())
   exposes it as /sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements.
U-Boot-only "tpm_event_log_addr/size" would work for U-Boot but leave
Linux without the log.  The region is reserved (but mapped) so Linux
does not reuse it before the TPM driver copies the log.
0x82400000/64KiB sits between U-Boot's TEXT_BASE (0x81200000) and
kernel_addr_r (0x84000000).

--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/qemu/qemu_rv64_virt_domain.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/qemu/qemu_rv64_virt_domain.dts
@@ -26,6 +26,24 @@
 		#address-cells = <0x01>;
 		#size-cells = <0x01>;
 		compatible = "qemu,platform\0simple-bus";
+
+		/* qemu: -device tpm-tis-device,tpmdev=tpm0 (backend: swtpm) */
+		tpm_tis@0 {
+			reg = <0x00 0x5000>;
+			compatible = "tcg,tpm-tis-mmio";
+			memory-region = <&tpm_event_log>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <0x02>;
+		#size-cells = <0x02>;
+		ranges;
+
+		/* TCG2 event log: written by U-Boot measured boot, read by Linux */
+		tpm_event_log: tpm-event-log@82400000 {
+			reg = <0x00 0x82400000 0x00 0x10000>;
+		};
 	};
 
 	memory@80000000 {
